module

public import Sheaf.RingTheory.ArtinianCoefficientPresentation

/-!
# Structure of finite coefficient subalgebras

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 07FG, proof of lemma-helper, the polynomial images.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-artinian-coefficient-subalgebra-structure).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

namespace Algebra

/-- The coefficient subalgebra is Artinian local, its maximal ideal is the contraction
of the ambient maximal ideal, and its residue field is the specified coefficient field.
No flatness or enlargement of the coefficient field is asserted here.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 07FG, proof of lemma-helper, the polynomial images, together
with the nilpotence argument in the proof of the coefficient claim.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-artinian-coefficient-subalgebra-structure).

Proof:
Put F=k(s) and let B be the subring generated by t and sigma(F). The containment
hypothesis makes B a k-subalgebra, hence B equals the stated algebra adjoin. Give B
its F-algebra structure by sigma restricted to F. The ambient maximal ideal is
nilpotent, say m^n=0. Since t lies in m, every monomial in t of total degree at least
n vanishes. The finitely many monomials of degree less than n span B over F; thus B
is finite dimensional over a field and Artinian. The ideal J generated by t in B
is nilpotent. Evaluation modulo J identifies B/J with F: every polynomial has its
constant term in sigma(F), and sigma(F) intersects J trivially, since applying the
ambient residue map to sigma(a) gives a while it kills J. For b=sigma(a)+h, h in J,
if a is nonzero then sigma(a) is invertible in B and the finite geometric series
in -sigma(a)^{-1}h inverts b. If a is zero then b is nilpotent and cannot be a unit
(B is nontrivial because it embeds in the local ring Λ). Thus the nonunits are
exactly J; B is local and J is its maximal ideal. Applying the ambient residue
map shows that J is precisely the contraction of m. The quotient isomorphism to
F respects k: reduction of the original structure map k -> Λ is the structure
map k -> K, regardless of whether sigma is a k-algebra map. -/
theorem artinian_coefficient_subalgebra_structure
    (k : Type u) [Field k] (Λ : Type v) [CommRing Λ] [Algebra k Λ]
    [IsArtinianRing Λ] [IsLocalRing Λ]
    (σ : IsLocalRing.ResidueField Λ →+* Λ) (t : Finset Λ)
    (s : Finset (IsLocalRing.ResidueField Λ))
    (hσ : (IsLocalRing.residue Λ).comp σ = RingHom.id _)
    (ht : (t : Set Λ) ⊆ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal Λ)
    (hc : ∀ a : k, algebraMap k Λ a ∈ Subring.closure ((t : Set Λ) ∪
      σ '' (IntermediateField.adjoin k (s : Set (IsLocalRing.ResidueField Λ)) :
        Set (IsLocalRing.ResidueField Λ)))) :
    ∃ hA : IsLocalRing (artinianCoefficientSubalgebra k Λ σ t s),
      IsArtinianRing (artinianCoefficientSubalgebra k Λ σ t s) ∧
      @IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (artinianCoefficientSubalgebra k Λ σ t s) _ hA =
        Ideal.comap (artinianCoefficientSubalgebra k Λ σ t s).val.toRingHom
          (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal Λ) ∧
      Nonempty (@IsLocalRing.ResidueField (artinianCoefficientSubalgebra k Λ σ t s) _ hA
        ≃ₐ[k] IntermediateField.adjoin k (s : Set (IsLocalRing.ResidueField Λ))) := by sorry

end Algebra
